== Upcoming Security Enhancements ==
=== Mounting Directories Securely ===
We are preparing to enhance system security by mounting important directories, such as <code>/home/user</code>, with the following options by default:
* <code>noexec</code>
* <code>nodev</code>
* <code>nosuid</code>

These options are designed to prevent the execution of binaries and scripts within these directories, reducing the risk of unauthorized or malicious code execution.

=== Restricting Compiler and Interpreter Access ===
[[File:Complimit-hp-promo.jpg|thumb]]
Access to compilers and interpreters will also be restricted to minimize the risk of malicious code compilation and execution. These restrictions are part of our proactive approach to security.

== Impact on Users and Workflows ==
While these measures will provide greater security, they may affect advanced users who rely on script execution in their home directories. We understand the potential for disruption and of course will provide options to opt-out.

=== Configuration ===
Secure mount options are applied at boot by the <code>remount-secure</code> service from the [[security-misc]] package. It remounts directories at runtime and requires '''no <code>/etc/fstab</code> changes''', so it behaves the same on Qubes and non-Qubes.

By default (highest level) <code>nosuid</code>, <code>nodev</code> and <code>noexec</code> are applied to user-writable data directories: <code>/home</code>, <code>/root</code>, <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/dev/shm</code>, <code>/srv</code>, <code>/media</code>, <code>/mnt</code>, <code>/var/tmp</code>, <code>/var/log</code>, <code>/var/cache</code> and <code>/boot</code>. <code>/var</code> keeps exec because <code>dpkg</code> runs package maintainer scripts from <code>/var/lib/dpkg/info</code>; <code>/usr</code>, <code>/etc</code> and <code>/opt</code> keep exec too.

The level is selected with the <code>remountsecure=</code> kernel parameter, configured in <code>/etc/default/grub.d/40_remount_secure.cfg</code>:

* <code>remountsecure=0</code> - disable.
* <code>remountsecure=1</code> - <code>nodev</code>, <code>nosuid</code> only.
* <code>remountsecure=2</code> - also <code>noexec</code> for most directories, '''excluding''' <code>/home</code>.
* <code>remountsecure=3</code> - also <code>noexec</code> for all, '''including''' <code>/home</code> (default, highest).

For example, to keep hardening but allow execution in <code>/home</code>, set <code>remountsecure=2</code> in that file, then run:

<pre>
sudo update-grub
</pre>

and reboot.

== Integration with Security Initiatives ==
These security improvements are integral to our broader security strategy, including:

* {{Check-todo}} [[Security Roadmap|Kicksecure Security Roadmap]]
* {{Check-yes}} [[Dev/Strong_Linux_User_Account_Isolation|Strong Linux User Account Isolation]]
* {{Check-yes}} [[SUID Disabler and Permission Hardener|SUID Disabler and Permission Hardener]]
* {{Check-todo}} [https://forums.whonix.org/t/lock-down-interpreters-compilers-interpreter-lock-compiler-lock/18499 interpreter lock / compiler lock]
* {{Check-yes}} [[Dev/user-sysmaint-split|user-sysmaint-split - Role-Based Boot Modes (user versus sysmaint) for Enhanced Security]]
* {{Check-todo}} [[Security-misc#Reduce_Kernel_Information_Leaks|Reduce Kernel Information Leaks]] (opt-in, goal is default enabled)
* {{Check-todo}} [[Security-misc#hidepid|hidepid]] (opt-in, goal is default enabled)

The goal is to fortify Linux user accounts against malware, making it difficult for a compromised account to affect others or to escape the virtual machine (VM) environment.

== Commitment to User Freedom ==
Our commitment to [[Reasons_for_Freedom_Software#No_Intentional_User_Freedom_Restrictions|No Intentional User Freedom Restrictions]] remains firm. Users retain the freedom to configure their systems as they see fit, in line with our core principles.

